Lots of upside potential, sure, but a decent chunk of the distribution is left of the
break-even line. 

Let's suppose the company cannot survive a loss of more than $300M. We can encode that
constraint as a loss-adjusted NPV function that is linear at ordinary outcomes (i.e.,
loss-adjusted NPV = NPV), but becomes hyperbolic as the result approaches the company
_oh shit_ threshold. The function below is capped at -$5B for the sake of plotting
clarity:

